UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 4: Presenting the report', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Turn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nExplain one fact about an Australian animal using a labelled diagram; name what your listener should notice.\n\nFEEDBACK\n''Look at the claws labelled on the drawing. They help this animal grip tree bark.'' The explanation adds meaning to the label.\n\nCHECK\nA slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPresent a 90-second report using your written report and one static illustration. Begin with a clear question or overview, explain three checked facts, refer accurately to the diagram, conclude, then assess voice, pace and audience understanding.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Turn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Present a 90-second report using your written report and one static illustration. Begin with a clear question or overview, explain three checked facts, refer accurately to the diagram, conclude, then assess voice, pace a Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: A report has a detailed paragraph about how bees pollinate flowers. In a talk I say, ''When bees visit flowers for nectar, pollen can move between blossoms,'' then point to labelled parts in my simple flower diagram.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation. Correction: ''Look at the claws labelled on the drawing. They help this animal grip tree bark.'' The explanation adds meaning to the label.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Turn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Turn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading.","A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make your 