 at the claws labelled on the drawing. They help this animal grip tree bark.'' The explanation adds meaning to the label.","A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Explain one fact about an Australian animal using a labelled diagram; name what \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Underline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"''Look at the claws labelled on the drawing. They help this animal grip tree bark.'' The explanation adds meaning to the label. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["''Look at the claws labelled on the drawing. They help this animal grip tree bark.'' The explanation adds meaning to the label.","A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a co\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Make one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant work with accurate reasoning and a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating the central skill, explaining the particular words or evidence that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: A report has a detailed paragraph about how bees pollinate flowers. In a talk I say, ''When bees visit flowers for nectar, pollen can move between blossoms,'' then point to labelled parts in my simple flower diagram.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change meaning or accuracy, not just make the text longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected example and purpose-linked explanation.","question":"Name one likely error in using this skill, correct it and explain why the correction helps.","sample_answer":"Common error: A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation. Correction: ''Look at the claws labelled on the drawing. They help this animal grip tree bark.'' The explanation adds meaning to the label.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w10-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise spoken report.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use spoken report with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify diagram reference and explain why it matters.","I can practise pace independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Turn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Turn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA report has a detailed paragraph about how bees pollinate flowers. In a talk I say, ''When bees visit flowers for nectar, pollen can move between blossoms,'' then point to labelled parts in my simple flower diagram.\n\nTRY FIRST\nExplain one fact about an Australian animal using a labelled diagram; name what your listener should notice.\n\nFEEDBACK\n''Look at the claws labelled on the drawing. They help this animal grip tree bark.'' The explanation adds meaning to the label.","explain":"Turn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Spoken bee fact linked to pointing at a labelled diagram.","caption":"Refer to the visual; do not read tiny slide text aloud.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Spoken point</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">pollen can move</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Visual</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">point to flower parts</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A report has a detailed paragraph about how bees pollinate flowers. In a talk I say, ''When bees visit flowers for nectar Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A report has a detailed paragraph about how bees pollinate flowers. In a talk I say, ''When bees visit flowers for nectar Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA report has a detailed paragraph about how bees pollinate flowers. In a talk I say, ''When bees visit flowers for nectar, pollen can move between blossoms,'' then point to labelled parts in my simple flower diagram. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Spoken report structure from overview to audience response.","caption":"Voice and pace matter as much as the written wording.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\"