oking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. A slide full of tiny text is not a substitute for an understandable spoken explanation. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTurn a written information report into a short spoken explanation with purposeful visual references, not a word-for-word reading. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 4: Presenting the report","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Information reports","week":10,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA report has a detailed paragraph about how bees pollinate flowers. In a talk I say, ''When bees visit flowers for nectar, pollen can move between blossoms,'' then point to labelled parts in my simple flower diagram.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Spoken bee fact linked to pointing at a labelled diagram.","caption":"Refer to the visual; do not read tiny slide text aloud.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Spoken point</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">pollen can move</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Visual</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">point to flower parts</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Spoken report structure from overview to audience response.","caption":"Voice and pace matter as much as the written wording.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height